course: history
lesson: 31
title: Slavery and its abolition
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Slavery, when people are owned by others and forced to work without pay or freedom, has happened in many places in history, but the Atlantic slave trade was one of the biggest crimes ever.
explain: From the 1500s to the 1800s European traders carried about 12 million Africans across the Atlantic by force to work on plantations of sugar, cotton and tobacco in the Americas.
explain: Enslaved people were never just victims: they kept their music, faith and stories alive, escaped, resisted, and in Haiti won a revolution that made the first free Black republic in 1804.
explain: Ordinary people helped too, like a whole school deciding not to buy one brand of sweets: in Britain thousands stopped buying slave-grown sugar and signed petitions until slavery was banned.
explain: In the USA slavery ended after the Civil War in 1865, but Black Americans still faced unfair laws until Rosa Parks, Martin Luther King Jr. and many others won civil rights in the 1960s.
fact: From the 1500s to the 1800s European traders took about 12 million Africans across the Atlantic by force to work as slaves in the Americas.
fact: In the triangle of trade, ships took goods from Europe to Africa, enslaved people from Africa to the Americas, and sugar, cotton and tobacco back to Europe.
fact: The voyage across the Atlantic was called the Middle Passage; people were chained and crowded into ships for weeks, and many died.
fact: Enslaved people worked on plantations without pay or freedom, and families could be sold apart, yet they kept music, faith, stories and families alive.
fact: Enslaved people resisted by working slowly, escaping and rising up, and in Haiti they won a revolution and made the first free Black republic in 1804.
fact: Olaudah Equiano, once enslaved, wrote his life story, and William Wilberforce argued in Parliament for years to end the slave trade.
fact: Britain banned the slave trade in 1807 and slavery in most of its empire in 1833.
fact: Abolitionists used badges with the words Am I not a man and a brother, sugar boycotts and petitions, and many ordinary people, including women, joined in.
fact: In the USA a secret network called the Underground Railroad helped escaping slaves travel north, using secret railway words such as stations and conductors, and Harriet Tubman escaped and went back many times to lead others to freedom.
fact: The American Civil War, from 1861 to 1865, was fought largely over slavery; Abraham Lincoln declared slaves in the rebel states free, and in 1865 slavery was ended in the whole USA.
fact: About four million people in the USA became free when slavery ended.
fact: In 1955 Rosa Parks refused to give up her bus seat to a white man, as an unfair law demanded, and Martin Luther King Jr. led peaceful marches and gave his I have a dream speech in 1963.
fact: New laws in the 1960s banned segregation, keeping Black and white people apart by law, and protected Black Americans' right to vote.
exercise: About how many Africans were taken across the Atlantic by force? | About 12 million | About 12 thousand | About 1,200 | About 12 billion
exercise: What were the three sides of the triangle of trade? | Europe to Africa, Africa to the Americas, the Americas to Europe | England to France to Spain | China to India to Japan | Australia to New Zealand to Hawaii
exercise: What was the Middle Passage? | The voyage of enslaved people across the Atlantic | A road through the Alps used by traders and their mules | A tunnel under London | A trade route in China
exercise: What did enslaved people grow on plantations? | Sugar, cotton and tobacco | Rice, tea and silk from China | Apples and pears only | Wheat for Europe's kings and queens
exercise: What did enslaved people keep alive despite everything? | Their music, faith, stories and families | Nothing at all, everything was taken from them on the ships | Only their shoes | Their castles
exercise: Which country was the first free Black republic? | Haiti | Brazil | Jamaica | Canada
exercise: What does abolition mean? | Ending something completely, here slavery | Building more and bigger ships for the trade | A type of sugar | Starting a new trade
exercise: Who was Olaudah Equiano? | A formerly enslaved man who wrote his life story | A Viking chief | A rich plantation owner who freed his own workers | A British king
exercise: Who argued in Parliament for years to end the slave trade? | William Wilberforce | Isaac Newton | Henry VIII | James Watt
exercise: When did Britain ban the slave trade? | 1807 | 1492 | 1066 | 1969
exercise: How could ordinary people help end slavery? | By refusing to buy slave-grown sugar and signing petitions | By buying more sugar | By staying quiet | By building plantations of their own and paying the workers a fair wage
exercise: What was the Underground Railroad? | A secret network helping escapers reach freedom | A real railway under the ground in London, with steam trains | A coal mine | A steam train company
exercise: Who led many people to freedom on the Underground Railroad? | Harriet Tubman | Marie Curie of Poland | Queen Victoria of Britain | Joan of Arc of France
exercise: When did slavery end in the whole USA? | 1865 | 1776 | 1492 | 1999
exercise: Which president declared slaves in the rebel states free? | Abraham Lincoln | George Washington | President Barack Obama | Winston Churchill
exercise: What did Rosa Parks refuse to do? | Give up her seat on a bus | Pay for her own bus ticket twice | Go to school | Vote
test: dude in the comments says enslaved people just sat there and accepted it. rubbish? | Rubbish, they resisted, escaped and even won a revolution in Haiti | True, they never resisted | True, they liked it | Rubbish, they all went home at once
test: Why did Britain's sugar boycott put pressure on slave owners? | Fewer people bought their sugar, so they earned less | Sugar became illegal everywhere in the world at once | It made sugar sweeter | It had no effect on anyone
test: If the Underground Railroad had no trains or tracks, why was it called a railroad? | It used railway words in secret, like stations and conductors | It really was a long train that ran under the ground | It was owned by a big railway company in New York | Nobody knows any reason
test: Which is true about slavery in history? | It happened in many societies, and the Atlantic trade was one of the largest | Only one country in the whole history of the world has ever had slavery | Slavery ended everywhere in 1492 | Slavery was always legal and fine
test: Why does Martin Luther King Jr.'s way of protest still inspire people? | He used peaceful marches and powerful words | He won a war with tanks | He became a king | He wrote the first computer program for the government
test: What would Rosa Parks staying seated on the bus show the world? | That unfair laws could be challenged peacefully | That buses were too full | That she was very tired of standing | Nothing important
test: four million ppl freed in 1865?? from what war | The American Civil War | The First World War in Europe | The Hundred Years' War in France | The Crusades to Jerusalem
test: Many European port cities grew rich in the 1700s. What is an uncomfortable part of that story? | Some of the money came from the slave trade | They found gold under the streets of the city long ago | They were paid by the Moon | It is not uncomfortable at all
test: Why did the words Am I not a man and a brother move so many people? | They asked people to see the enslaved person as an equal human | They were a funny joke | They advertised sugar | They were a song about football
test: After 1865 Black Americans were free. Why did they still need the civil rights movement? | Unfair laws still kept them apart and stopped many from voting | Everything was already equal, so there was nothing left to change | They wanted to bring slavery back | Nobody ever needed it
exam: Ascertain the approximate number of Africans forcibly transported across the Atlantic between the sixteenth and nineteenth centuries. | Twelve million | Twelve thousand | One hundred thousand | One billion
exam: Identify the first state to be established as a free Black republic, in eighteen hundred and four. | Haiti | Liberia | Brazil | Jamaica
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of enslaved people under slavery? | They resisted by diverse means, including escape and revolt | They acquiesced without any resistance | They were remunerated for their labour | They were free to depart at will
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the abolition of slavery throughout the United States. | It was accomplished in eighteen hundred and sixty-five | It was accomplished in seventeen hundred and seventy-six | It was accomplished in nineteen hundred and sixty-three | It has never been accomplished
